#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147198 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c3fbf3-5165-510f-b130-d216d5c8aa19" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1987" :question/problem "A computer has $10$ files infected with a computer virus. If the number of infected files quadruples every hour, find a function that gives the number of infected files after $t$ hours. How many infected files will there be after $6$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The number of infected files, $y(t)$, grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a494473-0da3-5789-a3da-08d8a0f62d6a" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" :question/math-academy-id "e-1988" :question/problem "In the week of opening, the grocery store sold $20$ kilograms of apples. After that, the weekly sales increased by $25 \\%$ every week.\n\nFind a function that models the total weight of apples sold in week $t$ after opening, and use this function to find the total amount of apples sold in the $4$th week." :question/worked-solution "The number of kilograms of apples sold $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $25 \\%$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 20$ is the total weight of the apples (in kilograms) sold initially\n- $r = 0.25$ is the growth rate ($25 \\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after opening\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny = 20 \\cdot (1 + 0.25)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the total weight of apples sold in week $4$, we evaluate the above for $t=4$:\n\n$$\ny(4) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{4} \\approx 49\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $49$ kilograms of apples were sold in week $4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147215 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/id #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21183" :question/problem "A podcast was released with $80$ initial listeners. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after release, the number of listeners increased by $190\\%$ of the number of listeners at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many listeners are there estimated to be after $2$ weeks?" :question/worked-solution "The number of listeners $L(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190\\%$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 80$ is the total number of listeners initially\n- $r = 1.9$ is the growth rate ($190\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nL = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $L(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL = 80 \\cdot (1 + 1.9)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nL(t) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of listeners after $2$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\nL(2) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{2} \\approx 673\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of listeners after $2$ weeks is $673$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb2a01a6-39e9-55ac-ac4a-eaab72e72257" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Exponential Growth Using Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "907d6a8b-220d-5ffa-a86f-09363e7e2f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3859" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144304} {:answer/id #uuid "94778e61-ef88-5a09-9df7-8ee5df28bf58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3685" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144305} {:answer/id #uuid "ea98df17-9ef1-5762-bfe2-bd4e457076aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144306} {:answer/id #uuid "430728e2-b29f-55c2-a057-2541cf308593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3754" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144307} {:answer/id #uuid "46941793-8685-5dbd-ab7e-f7fa10af6e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1103" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144308}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb9ea82-bb25-57e8-9225-b8bc7341a2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144303}] :question/id #uuid "66005c3f-ae8d-568e-8ad1-166663174f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-318220" :question/problem "A concert video was uploaded with $90$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after upload, the number of views increased by $250%$ of the number of views at 